  "body": "Follow-up to #6. A delayed audit report surfaced a defect that #6 missed, and it is the most severe one in the rotation engine. 4.14.0 -> 4.14.1 (PATCH).\n\n## The bug\n\nSub-providers classify their own SDK/HTTP failures **before** `RotatingProvider` sees them, so an upstream 401/403 arrives as `ExecutionFailure(retryable=False)` — not a raw `openai.AuthenticationError` or `httpx.HTTPStatusError`. `error_justifies_rotation` recognized only the raw shapes, and `retryable=False` made both fallback checks decline:\n\n- not `openai.AuthenticationError`, not `httpx.HTTPStatusError` → first two checks fail\n- `retryable_transient_status`: `exc.retryable and ...` → `None`\n- `retryable_upstream_transport_error`: `exc.retryable and ...` → `False`\n\nSo it returned `False` for the exact failure rotation exists to handle. **A revoked, expired, or exhausted key failed the in-flight request outright instead of failing over to a healthy credential.**\n\nVerified against the real classifier (`classify_provider_failure`) before the fix:\n\n```\n401 invalid key    kind=upstream     retryable=False  ROTATE=False   <-- bug\n403 forbidden      kind=upstream     retryable=False  ROTATE=False   <-- bug\n429 rate limit     kind=rate_limit   retryable=True   ROTATE=True\n503 overload       kind=upstream     retryable=True   ROTATE=True\n```\n\nNote 401 classifies to `kind=upstream`, not `AUTHENTICATION` — so keying off `FailureKind` would not have caught it either. The fix keys off the status code.\n\n## Scope of impact\n\nNot total breakage: `report_failure` still ran, so the key was benched `LOCKED_OUT` and *subsequent* requests routed around it. But every credential death cost one user-visible request failure — precisely what failover is meant to prevent, and worse under `failover` policy where the whole point is transparent recovery.\n\n## The fix\n\n`retryable` encodes \"safe to retry the **same** credential\", which a 401 never is. It says nothing about whether a *different* credential would succeed. Rotation now keys off the auth status directly.\n\nAfter:\n\n```\n401 invalid key    ROTATE=True\n403 forbidden      ROTATE=True\n429 rate limit     ROTATE=True\n503 overload       ROTATE=True\n400 bad request    ROTATE=False   <-- still correct, fails identically on every key\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n- Parametrized regression test over 401/403 asserting `retryable is False` **and** rotation is allowed, so the discrepancy that caused this can't silently return.\n- `test_classified_bad_request_still_does_not_rotate` guards against over-rotating.\n- End-to-end: a 401 on key 0 is served by key 1, key 0 ends `LOCKED_OUT`.\n- Full `./scripts/ci.sh`: **3017 passed, 14 failed** — exactly the known pre-existing set (websearch tests reading the real `~/.fcc/.env`, PowerShell installer checksum). **Zero new failures.**",
