    "message": "fix(rotation): fail over a rejected credential instead of failing the request\n\nProviders classify their own SDK/HTTP failures before the rotating wrapper\nsees them, so an upstream 401/403 arrives as ExecutionFailure(retryable=False)\nrather than a raw openai/httpx error. ``error_justifies_rotation`` recognized\nonly the raw shapes, and ``retryable=False`` made both fallback checks decline,\nso it returned False for the one failure rotation exists to handle.\n\nThe result: a revoked, expired, or exhausted key failed the in-flight request\noutright instead of failing over to a healthy credential. Health tracking still\nbenched the key, so later requests routed around it, but every credential death\ncost a user-visible failure -- exactly what failover is supposed to prevent.\n\n``retryable`` encodes \"safe to retry the same credential\", which a 401 never\nis; it says nothing about whether a *different* credential would succeed.\nRotation now keys off the auth status directly. A 400 still does not rotate,\nsince it fails identically on every key.\n\nVerified against the real classifier: 401/403 now rotate, 429/503 still do,\n400 still does not, plus an end-to-end test that a 401 on key 0 is served by\nkey 1 and leaves key 0 LOCKED_OUT.",
